uld I init pipeline state? The CLAUDE.md mentions tmp/pipeline-state.yaml constraint about wait-for-wave — that's for the orchestrator launching waves of agents; I'm a single decompose agent, not launching agent waves. The prompt (decompose-agent.md) doesn't mention state.py or wait-for-wave. I'm the agent launched by the pipeline; I don't launch waves. So no.\n\nAlso check: does `python3 scripts/frontmatter.py set` need the file to exist? Yes — write body first with Write tool, then set frontmatter. The body written without frontmatter delimiters; frontmatter.py set presumably prepends/creates frontmatter. Let me verify how set behaves on a file without frontmatter — probably fine.\n\nLet me now write the decomposition summary.\n\nComponent names double-check from canonical list (verbatim): \"Serving Orchestration\" ✓ (line 78), \"INFERENG Midstream\" ✓ (line 36), \"Documentation\" ✓ (line 30).\n\nTitles:\n- E001: \"Assess upstream standalone KEDA PR readiness and OpenShift adaptation needs\"\n- E002: \"Standalone KEDA autoscaling path in the KServe llmisvc-controller\"\n- E003: \"KEDA TriggerAuthentication reconciler for LLMInferenceService in odh-model-controller\"\n- E004: \"Autoscaling documentation: both paths, decision guide, and troubleshooting\"\n\nNow, the bodies. Must have sections: Title (one line), Description, Scope, Acceptance Criteria, HLR Traceability. No sibling epic references. For gated epic E003, describe dependency in plain terms: \"requires the LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA API (spec.scaling.keda) to be available\" and \"the upstream adaptation assessment\".\n\nLet me write all files now. I'll use Write for bodies then frontmatter.py set via Bash.\n\nE001 body (Investigation):\n\n# Investigate upstream standalone KEDA readiness and OpenShift adaptation needs\n\n## Description\nResolve the strategy's open question of whether the upstream implementation of standalone KEDA autoscaling for LLMInferenceService (kserve/kserve#5839, implementing accepted design kserve/kserve#5646) requires downstream adaptation beyond the planned KEDA TriggerAuthentication flow in odh-model-controller. The outcome determines the exact scope of the OpenShift-side reconciliation work (potential RBAC and TLS adjustments) and confirms the upstream API surface matches the assumptions this strategy builds on.\n\n## Scope\n- Review the open upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 and the accepted design kserve/kserve#5646 against the strategy's assumptions: standalone KEDA spec fields at the ScalingSpec level (pollingInterval, cooldownPeriod, triggers), CEL validation relaxed from requiring wva to at-least-one/at-most-one of wva or keda, controller branching that creates ScaledObjects and skips VariantAutoscaling CR creation.\n- Enumerate every OpenShift-specific downstream adaptation required beyond the odh-model-controller TriggerAuthentication flow — in particular potential RBAC and TLS adjustments — and identify the target repository for each.\n- Ground each finding in the existing reference implementations and architecture context: the v1beta1 InferenceService KEDA reconciler (pkg/controller/v1beta1/inferenceservice/reconcilers/keda/keda_reconciler.go), the odh-model-controller kserve_keda_reconciler, and the kserve.md / odh-model-controller.md architecture context files.\n- Recommend a landing strategy (wait for upstream merge versus carrying the patch downstream via the midstream fork with backport), with rationale, for the Serving team.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A written assessment enumerates, for each element of the upstream PR's API surface, whether it matches the accepted design and the assumptions stated in this strategy (pass: every element classified as matching or diverging, with the divergence described).\n- The assessment lists a complete, individually-justified inventory of required OpenShift downstream adaptations beyond the TriggerAuthentication flow, each with a target repository and recommended approach (pass: inventory exists and each item cites its evidence from the PR diff, reference implementation, or architecture context).\n- A landing recommendation (upstream-merge versus downstream carry) is delivered with rationale covering the PR's current state (pass: recommendation present, evidence-based).\n- Findings are communicated to the Serving team in a form usable to scope the odh-model-controller reconciliation work (pass: recorded deliverable, e.g. assessment document or ticket comment).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- No HLR is directly implemented by this investigation. It resolves the strategy's open question \"Does the upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 require downstream adaptation beyond authentication context?\" (owner: KServe team), which gates the OpenShift delivery of HLR-1 (standalone KEDA autoscaling with user-defined triggers) and shapes the scope of the authentication-context work it depends on.\n\nHmm wait — \"the authentication-context work it depends on\" — fine, no epic IDs. Good.\n\nE002 body:\n\n# Standalone KEDA autoscaling path in the KServe llmisvc-controller\n\n## Description\nImplement standalone KEDA autoscaling as a first-class path for LLMInferenceService in the KServe llmisvc-controller, following the accepted upstream design (kserve/kserve#5646) and its implementation PR (kserve/kserve#5839): users configure KEDA triggers directly in the LLMInferenceService spec without deploying WVA. This covers the API types, CEL validation, controller branching, and scaling status conditions for both autoscaling paths, and landing the change upstream (or carrying it downstream as fallback).\n\n## Scope\n- API type changes in pkg/apis/serving/v1alpha2/llm_inference_service_types.go: standalone KEDA spec at the ScalingSpec level with pollingInterval, cooldownPeriod, and a triggers field for user-defined KEDA trigger definitions (type, metadata map).\n- Validation changes in pkg/apis/serving/v1alpha2/llm_inference_service_validation.go: relax the CEL rule from requiring wva to requiring at least one of wva or keda, enforce mutual exclusivity between the two, validate trigger completeness, and keep the scaling configuration mutually exclusive with static spec.replicas.\n- For disaggregated deployments, enforce that prefill and decode phases use the same scaling path (actuator consistency constraint from overlay 0011).\n- Controller changes in pkg/controller/v1alpha2/llmisvc/scaling.go: branch on the configured path — standalone KEDA creates a ScaledObject from user-provided triggers (Resource, External, and PodMetric trigger types per the v1beta1 reference reconciler at pkg/controller/v1beta1/inferenceservice/reconcilers/keda/keda_reconciler.go) and skips VariantAutoscaling CR creation entirely.\n- Scale-to-zero via KEDA idleReplicaCount: 0 and scale-from-zero via user-configured triggers (e.g. Prometheus queries on EPP flow control queue metrics); phase-specific ScaledObjects for independent prefill/decode scaling in disaggregated deployments.\n- Scaling status conditions and KEDA ScaledObject event emission for the standalone path (signal attribution for operators).\n- Regression safety: existing WVA-mediated autoscaling and static deployments (no spec.scaling) behave identically to before.\n- Landing: drive the change to merge in upstream kserve (PR #5839 or successor); if upstream acceptance is delayed, carry the patch via the midstream/downstream fork with a backport plan once merged.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Given an LLMInferenceService with spec.scaling.keda configured with user-defined triggers and no WVA deployed, when a trigger condition is met (e.g. a Prometheus query threshold is exceeded), then the deployment scales up — measured by KEDA ScaledObject status showing active and Deployment replica count increasing.\n- Given an LLMInferenceService with both spec.scaling.wva and spec.scaling.keda set, when the CR is submitted, then the API server rejects it with a CEL validation error indicating mutual exclusivity.\n- Given an LLMInferenceService with spec.scaling.keda and no spec.scaling.wva, when the llmisvc-controller reconciles, then no VariantAutoscaling CR is created and a ScaledObject with the user-provided triggers is created — measured by kubectl get variantautoscalings returning empty and kubectl get scaledobjects returning a ScaledObject with the user's triggers.\n- Given an LLMInferenceService with KEDA and idleReplicaCount: 0, when no requests are received for the cooldown period, then the deployment scales to zero — measured by Deployment replica count reaching 0 and KEDA ScaledObject status showing idle; and a subsequent trigger scales it back up from zero to serving (validates the scale-from-zero assumption for LLM inference workloads).\n- Given a disaggregated LLMInferenceService with separate scaling configs, when prefill pods reach saturation while decode pods remain below threshold, then only the prefill pool scales up — measured by independent scaling resource status for prefill and decode roles; and a CR configuring different scaling paths for the two phases is rejected by validation.\n- Given a deployment with autoscaling active, when Prometheus metrics become unavailable, then the deployment holds its current replica count — measured by replica count remaining stable during the metric outage.\n- Static deployments with no autoscaling configured continue to serve identically — measured by no VariantAutoscaling, HPA, or ScaledObject resources created for deployments without spec.scaling, and by the existing WVA-path test suite passing unchanged.\n- Scaling events for the standalone path are emitted as KEDA ScaledObject events with signal attribution.\n- The standalone KEDA support is landed in upstream kserve, or carried in the midstream/downstream fork with a documented backport plan (fallback if upstream acceptance is delayed).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-1 [P0]: standalone KEDA autoscaling with user-defined triggers without requiring WVA deployment — API, validation, and controller implementation.\n- HLR-2 [P0]: mutual exclusivity of the two autoscaling paths with CEL at-least-one / at-most-one enforcement.\n- HLR-3 [P0]: scale-to-zero via KEDA idleReplicaCount: 0 (both paths).\n- HLR-4 [P0]: scale-from-zero — standalone KEDA via user-configured triggers (the WVA engine already covers the WVA path).\n- HLR-5 [P0]: independent prefill/decode pool scaling in disaggregated deployments.\n- HLR-6 [P1]: scaling events visible to operators with signal attribution (KEDA ScaledObject events for the standalone path) — bundled here because it falls out of the same ScaledObject creation surface.\n\nE003 body:\n\n# KEDA TriggerAuthentication reconciler for LLMInferenceService in odh-model-controller\n\n## Description\nExtend odh-model-controller with a reconciliation flow that creates the per-namespace KEDA authentication context for LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA Prometheus-type triggers on OpenShift, so KEDA ScaledObjects can query Thanos/Prometheus with an authenticated context. Follows the existing kserve_keda_reconciler pattern used for v1beta1 InferenceService KEDA autoscaling.\n\n## Scope\n- A parallel reconciler flow to the existing kserve_keda_reconciler (internal/controller/serving/reconcilers/kserve_keda_reconciler.go) covering LLMInferenceService resources configured for standalone KEDA with Prometheus-type triggers.\n- Create the same authentication context as the v1beta1 flow: a dedicated ServiceAccount, a ServiceAccountToken Secret, a Role/RoleBinding for metrics access, and a KEDA TriggerAuthentication CR supplying bearer token and CA cert to KEDA's Prometheus triggers.\n- Follow the reference implementation for resource naming, owner reference management, and lifecycle cleanup.\n- Implement any OpenShift-specific downstream adaptations (e.g. RBAC or TLS adjustments) identified by the upstream adaptation assessment, in this repository or the identified target repository as appropriate.\n- No changes to the existing v1beta1 kserve_keda_reconciler behavior.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Given an LLMInferenceService configured for standalone KEDA with a Prometheus-type trigger on OpenShift, when the controller reconciles, then the per-namespace authentication context (ServiceAccount, ServiceAccountToken Secret, Role/RoleBinding, KEDA TriggerAuthentication) is created — measured by the resources existing and the KEDA Prometheus trigger authenticating successfully against Thanos/Prometheus.\n- Given an LLMInferenceService using the authentication context is deleted, then the created resources are cleaned up via owner references — measured by no orphaned authentication resources remaining.\n- Given LLMInferenceService resources without standalone KEDA Prometheus triggers, then no authentication resources are created for them — measured by absence of the resources in those namespaces.\n- The existing v1beta1 InferenceService KEDA authentication flow continues to behave identically — measured by its test suite passing unchanged.\n- Downstream adaptations identified by the upstream adaptation assessment are implemented, or tracked with an agreed owner if they belong outside this repository — measured by each assessment finding dispositioned.\n- Prometheus/Thanos queries from KEDA use ServiceAccount token with TLS 1.2+ via the TriggerAuthentication CR.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-1 [P0]: standalone KEDA autoscaling with user-defined triggers without requiring WVA — delivers the authenticated Prometheus trigger path on OpenShift, the primary supported standalone trigger source.\n\nE004 body:\n\n# Autoscaling documentation: both paths, decision guide, and troubleshooting\n\n## Description\nAuthor the user-facing autoscaling documentation for LLMInferenceService covering both autoscaling paths (WVA-mediated and standalone KEDA) plus static allocation: a decision guide, configuration recipes per workload shape, troubleshooting, and a supported configuration matrix, including KEDA as a documented prerequisite.\n\n## Scope (content outline inside Scope)\n- Decision guide: WVA-mediated autoscaling vs. standalone KEDA vs. static allocation, with use-case-based recommendations.\n- Configuration recipes per workload shape, including the disaggregated prefill/decode topology and the coding-assistant example scenario (business-hours scaling, overnight GPU release).\n- Standalone KEDA reference: spec.scaling.keda fields (pollingInterval, cooldownPeriod, triggers), mutual exclusivity rules and CEL error messages, and instructions to reference the TriggerAuthentication resource in KEDA trigger definitions for authenticated Prometheus/Thanos queries on OpenShift.\n- Scale-to-zero and scale-from-zero guidance: KEDA as required actuator backend for scale-to-zero, recommended scale-from-zero trigger configurations using EPP flow control queue metrics, and example ScaledObject templates.\n- KEDA as a documented prerequisite with installation guidance referencing the GitOps repository (ODH-ADR-0007) — the platform does not auto-install KEDA (overlay 0008).\n- Realistic scale-from-zero (cold start) latency expectations and headroom recommendations, incorporating the agreed p50/p95 scale-from-zero targets once available.\n- Prometheus capacity guidance for fleet-scale deployments, incorporating Performance/PSAP team input.\n- Supported configuration matrix across both paths and topologies; troubleshooting paths for misconfigured triggers and failed zero-to-one transitions.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Documentation covers both autoscaling paths and static allocation with a decision guide, per-workload-shape configuration recipes, troubleshooting, and a supported configuration matrix.\n- Standalone KEDA instructions include referencing the TriggerAuthentication resource for authenticated Prometheus/Thanos queries on OpenShift, and KEDA installation as a prerequisite with GitOps-referenced guidance.\n- Recommended scale-from-zero trigger configurations using