{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-9901da05-e242-48ad-b9e3-6a44bc11c0d4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-bd7d87bb42fff141","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8008-wfar2syk/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8008-E003.md","content":"# KEDA TriggerAuthentication reconciler for LLMInferenceService in odh-model-controller\n\n## Description\n\nExtend odh-model-controller with a reconciliation flow that creates the per-namespace KEDA authentication context for LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA Prometheus-type triggers on OpenShift, so KEDA ScaledObjects can query Thanos/Prometheus with an authenticated context. The flow follows the existing `kserve_keda_reconciler` pattern that already provides this for v1beta1 InferenceService KEDA autoscaling.\n\n## Scope\n\n- A reconciler flow parallel to the existing `kserve_keda_reconciler` (at `internal/controller/serving/reconcilers/kserve_keda_reconciler.go`) covering LLMInferenceService resources configured for standalone KEDA with Prometheus-type triggers.\n- Create the same authentication context as the v1beta1 flow: a dedicated ServiceAccount, a ServiceAccountToken Secret, a Role/RoleBinding for metrics access, and a KEDA TriggerAuthentication CR supplying bearer token and CA cert to KEDA's Prometheus triggers.\n- Follow the existing reconciler as the reference implementation for resource naming, owner reference management, and lifecycle cleanup.\n- Implement any OpenShift-specific downstream adaptations (e.g. RBAC or TLS adjustments) identified by the upstream adaptation assessment, in this repository or in the target repository the assessment assigns them to.\n- No changes to the existing v1beta1 `kserve_keda_reconciler` behavior.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given an LLMInferenceService configured for standalone KEDA with a Prometheus-type trigger on OpenShift, when the controller reconciles, then the per-namespace authentication context (dedicated ServiceAccount, ServiceAccountToken Secret, Role/RoleBinding, KEDA TriggerAuthentication CR) is created — measured by the resources existing and the KEDA Prometheus trigger authenticating successfully against Thanos/Prometheus.\n- Given an LLMInferenceService using this authentication context is deleted, then the created resources are cleaned up through owner references — measured by no orphaned authentication resources remaining.\n- Given LLMInferenceService resources without standalone KEDA Prometheus triggers, then no authentication resources are created for them — measured by absence of the resources in those namespaces.\n- The existing v1beta1 InferenceService KEDA authentication flow continues to behave identically — measured by its test suite passing unchanged.\n- Every downstream adaptation identified by the upstream adaptation assessment is implemented, or tracked with an agreed owner where the assessment assigns it to another repository — measured by each assessment finding having a disposition.\n- Prometheus/Thanos queries from KEDA use the ServiceAccount token with TLS 1.2+ via the TriggerAuthentication CR.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 [P0]: LLMInferenceService supports standalone KEDA autoscaling with user-defined triggers without requiring WVA deployment — this epic delivers the authenticated Prometheus trigger path on OpenShift, the primary supported standalone KEDA trigger source for inference metrics.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":60780,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-849512a184fbd334","agent_id":"a512e7cd3c323ed69","session_id":"04280142-99a3-46b0-81af-b1932074d3a4","uuid":"6e3c4139-3a2f-4fde-ad19-0ca9f259d170","timestamp":"2026-10-22T05:14:39.434Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8008"}
