{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8723f593d2113722","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8008-E001\n3\ttitle: Assess upstream standalone KEDA PR readiness and OpenShift adaptation needs\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8008\n5\tcomponent: Serving Orchestration\n6\tteam: Serving\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tinvestigation_signals:\n10\t  question_specificity: 1\n11\t  source_accessibility: 1\n12\t  local_runnability: 0\n13\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: 0\n14\t  human_judgment_required: -1\n15\timplementation_type: null\n16\tdependencies: []\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: Medium\n19\tai_implementability_score: 1\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# Assess upstream standalone KEDA PR readiness and OpenShift adaptation needs\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tResolve the strategy's open question of whether the upstream implementation of standalone KEDA autoscaling for LLMInferenceService (kserve/kserve#5839, implementing the accepted design kserve/kserve#5646) requires downstream adaptation beyond the planned KEDA TriggerAuthentication flow in odh-model-controller. The outcome determines the exact scope of the OpenShift-side reconciliation work (in particular potential RBAC and TLS adjustments) and confirms that the upstream API surface matches the assumptions this strategy builds on.\n30\t\n31\t## Scope\n32\t\n33\t- Review the open upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 and the accepted design kserve/kserve#5646 against the strategy's assumptions: a standalone KEDA spec at the ScalingSpec level (`pollingInterval`, `cooldownPeriod`, `triggers` for user-defined KEDA trigger definitions), CEL validation relaxed from requiring `wva` to at-least-one / at-most-one of `wva` or `keda`, and controller branching that creates a ScaledObject from user-provided triggers while skipping VariantAutoscaling CR creation.\n34\t- Enumerate every OpenShift-specific downstream adaptation required beyond the odh-model-controller TriggerAuthentication flow — in particular potential RBAC and TLS adjustments — and identify the target repository for each.\n35\t- Ground each finding in the existing reference implementations and architecture context: the v1beta1 InferenceService KEDA reconciler (`pkg/controller/v1beta1/inferenceservice/reconcilers/keda/keda_reconciler.go`), the odh-model-controller `kserve_keda_reconciler`, and the kserve / odh-model-controller architecture context files.\n36\t- Recommend a landing strategy (wait for upstream merge versus carrying the patch downstream via the midstream fork with a backport once merged), with rationale, for the Serving team.\n37\t\n38\t## Acceptance Criteria\n39\t\n40\t- A written assessment classifies every element of the upstream PR's API surface as matching or diverging from the accepted design and the assumptions stated in this strategy, with each divergence described.\n41\t- The assessment contains a complete inventory of required OpenShift downstream adaptations beyond the TriggerAuthentication flow, each item individually justified with evidence from the PR diff, the reference implementations, or architecture context, and each assigned a target repository and recommended approach.\n42\t- A landing recommendation (upstream merge versus downstream carry) is delivered with rationale covering the PR's current state.\n43\t- The findings are recorded in a form the Serving team can use to finalize the scope of the odh-model-controller reconciliation work.\n44\t\n45\t## HLR Traceability\n46\t\n47\t- No HLR is directly implemented by this investigation. It resolves the strategy's open question \"Does the upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 require downstream adaptation beyond authentication context?\" (owner: KServe team), which gates the OpenShift delivery of HLR-1 (standalone KEDA autoscaling with user-defined triggers) and shapes the scope of the authentication-context work that HLR-1 depends on.\n48\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-83d800cd5acabd92","agent_id":"aecb55fe7396d6010","session_id":"04280142-99a3-46b0-81af-b1932074d3a4","uuid":"48e01c46-da0e-4e15-836d-6ef942af10e2","timestamp":"2026-10-22T05:16:24.154Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8723f593d2113722","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8008 decomposition"}
