{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-889194b36730aac3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8008-E003\n3\ttitle: KEDA TriggerAuthentication reconciler for LLMInferenceService in odh-model-controller\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8008\n5\tcomponent: INFERENG Midstream\n6\tteam: Serving\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8008-E001\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8008-E002\n12\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8008-E001\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: add_remediation\n15\t  fallback_approach: If the adaptation assessment identifies OpenShift-specific adaptations\n16\t    beyond the TriggerAuthentication flow (e.g. RBAC or TLS adjustments), the additional\n17\t    work is added to this epic's scope or tracked with the assigned target repository;\n18\t    the epic itself is not removed or restructured.\n19\tai_signals:\n20\t  change_specificity: 1\n21\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n22\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n23\t  existing_foundation: 1\n24\t  open_questions: -1\n25\t  external_dependency: -1\n26\t  human_process_gates: 0\n27\t  repo_access: 1\n28\t  architecture_claims: 1\n29\timplementation_type: null\n30\tinvestigation_signals: null\n31\tai_implementability: High\n32\tai_implementability_score: 4\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\tbranch: null\n35\t---\n36\t# KEDA TriggerAuthentication reconciler for LLMInferenceService in odh-model-controller\n37\t\n38\t## Description\n39\t\n40\tExtend odh-model-controller with a reconciliation flow that creates the per-namespace KEDA authentication context for LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA Prometheus-type triggers on OpenShift, so KEDA ScaledObjects can query Thanos/Prometheus with an authenticated context. The flow follows the existing `kserve_keda_reconciler` pattern that already provides this for v1beta1 InferenceService KEDA autoscaling.\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\t- A reconciler flow parallel to the existing `kserve_keda_reconciler` (at `internal/controller/serving/reconcilers/kserve_keda_reconciler.go`) covering LLMInferenceService resources configured for standalone KEDA with Prometheus-type triggers.\n45\t- Create the same authentication context as the v1beta1 flow: a dedicated ServiceAccount, a ServiceAccountToken Secret, a Role/RoleBinding for metrics access, and a KEDA TriggerAuthentication CR supplying bearer token and CA cert to KEDA's Prometheus triggers.\n46\t- Follow the existing reconciler as the reference implementation for resource naming, owner reference management, and lifecycle cleanup.\n47\t- Implement any OpenShift-specific downstream adaptations (e.g. RBAC or TLS adjustments) identified by the upstream adaptation assessment, in this repository or in the target repository the assessment assigns them to.\n48\t- No changes to the existing v1beta1 `kserve_keda_reconciler` behavior.\n49\t\n50\t## Acceptance Criteria\n51\t\n52\t- Given an LLMInferenceService configured for standalone KEDA with a Prometheus-type trigger on OpenShift, when the controller reconciles, then the per-namespace authentication context (dedicated ServiceAccount, ServiceAccountToken Secret, Role/RoleBinding, KEDA TriggerAuthentication CR) is created — measured by the resources existing and the KEDA Prometheus trigger authenticating successfully against Thanos/Prometheus.\n53\t- Given an LLMInferenceService using this authentication context is deleted, then the created resources are cleaned up through owner references — measured by no orphaned authentication resources remaining.\n54\t- Given LLMInferenceService resources without standalone KEDA Prometheus triggers, then no authentication resources are created for them — measured by absence of the resources in those namespaces.\n55\t- The existing v1beta1 InferenceService KEDA authentication flow continues to behave identically — measured by its test suite passing unchanged.\n56\t- Every downstream adaptation identified by the upstream adaptation assessment is implemented, or tracked with an agreed owner where the assessment assigns it to another repository — measured by each assessment finding having a disposition.\n57\t- Prometheus/Thanos queries from KEDA use the ServiceAccount token with TLS 1.2+ via the TriggerAuthentication CR.\n58\t\n59\t## HLR Traceability\n60\t\n61\t- HLR-1 [P0]: LLMInferenceService supports standalone KEDA autoscaling with user-defined triggers without requiring WVA deployment — this epic delivers the authenticated Prometheus trigger path on OpenShift, the primary supported standalone KEDA trigger source for inference metrics.\n62\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-83d800cd5acabd92","agent_id":"aecb55fe7396d6010","session_id":"04280142-99a3-46b0-81af-b1932074d3a4","uuid":"05130408-41da-4733-810d-db0ebfc26ce5","timestamp":"2026-10-22T05:16:31.385Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-889194b36730aac3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8008 decomposition"}
