{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a789a4d74fa1815c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8008-E004\n3\ttitle: 'Autoscaling documentation: both paths, decision guide, and troubleshooting'\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8008\n5\tcomponent: Documentation\n6\tteam: Documentation\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\timplementation_type: docs-authoring\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tdependencies:\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8008-E002\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8008-E003\n13\tai_signals:\n14\t  change_specificity: 1\n15\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n16\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n17\t  existing_foundation: 1\n18\t  open_questions: -1\n19\t  external_dependency: 0\n20\t  human_process_gates: 0\n21\t  repo_access: 1\n22\t  architecture_claims: 1\n23\tinvestigation_signals: null\n24\tai_implementability: High\n25\tai_implementability_score: 4\n26\tjira_key: null\n27\tbranch: null\n28\tgated_by: null\n29\tgate_failure_impact: null\n30\t---\n31\t# Autoscaling documentation: both paths, decision guide, and troubleshooting\n32\t\n33\t## Description\n34\t\n35\tAuthor the user-facing autoscaling documentation for LLMInferenceService covering both autoscaling paths (WVA-mediated and standalone KEDA) plus static allocation: a decision guide, configuration recipes per workload shape, troubleshooting, and a supported configuration matrix, including KEDA as a documented prerequisite.\n36\t\n37\t## Scope\n38\t\n39\tContent outline:\n40\t\n41\t- Decision guide: WVA-mediated autoscaling vs. standalone KEDA vs. static allocation, with use-case-based recommendations (including the coding-assistant scenario of scaling prefill and decode pools during business hours and releasing GPUs overnight).\n42\t- Configuration recipes per workload shape, including the disaggregated prefill/decode topology.\n43\t- Standalone KEDA reference: the `spec.scaling.keda` fields (`pollingInterval`, `cooldownPeriod`, `triggers`), the mutual exclusivity rules and their CEL error messages, and instructions to reference the TriggerAuthentication resource in KEDA trigger definitions for authenticated Prometheus/Thanos queries on OpenShift.\n44\t- Scale-to-zero and scale-from-zero guidance: KEDA as the required actuator backend for scale-to-zero, recommended scale-from-zero trigger configurations using EPP flow control queue metrics, and example ScaledObject templates.\n45\t- KEDA as a documented prerequisite with installation guidance referencing the GitOps repository (ODH-ADR-0007) — the platform does not auto-install KEDA (overlay 0008).\n46\t- Realistic scale-from-zero (cold start) latency expectations and headroom recommendations, incorporating the agreed p50/p95 scale-from-zero targets once available.\n47\t- Prometheus capacity guidance for fleet-scale deployments, incorporating Performance/PSAP team input.\n48\t- Supported configuration matrix across both paths and topologies; troubleshooting for misconfigured triggers and failed zero-to-one transitions.\n49\t\n50\t## Acceptance Criteria\n51\t\n52\t- Documentation covers both autoscaling paths and static allocation with a decision guide, per-workload-shape configuration recipes, troubleshooting, and a supported configuration matrix.\n53\t- The standalone KEDA instructions include referencing the TriggerAuthentication resource for authenticated Prometheus/Thanos queries on OpenShift, and KEDA installation as a prerequisite with GitOps-referenced guidance.\n54\t- Recommended scale-from-zero trigger configurations using EPP flow control queue metrics are documented, with example ScaledObject templates.\n55\t- Scale-from-zero latency expectations, headroom recommendations, and fleet-scale Prometheus capacity guidance are included, reflecting the inputs received from the owning teams for those open questions; where an input is still pending, best-available values are documented and explicitly flagged as pending.\n56\t- The documentation is technically reviewed against the implemented behavior of both autoscaling paths and validated against the architecture context (kserve, odh-model-controller, and workload-variant-autoscaler architecture context files).\n57\t\n58\t## HLR Traceability\n59\t\n60\t- HLR-7 [P1]: well-lit path documentation covering both autoscaling paths with decision guide, configuration recipes per workload shape, and troubleshooting.\n61\t- HLR-3 [P0]: scale-to-zero — configuration guidance for KEDA `idleReplicaCount: 0`.\n62\t- HLR-4 [P0]: scale-from-zero — recommended trigger configurations using EPP queue metrics and example templates.\n63\t- HLR-5 [P0]: disaggregated prefill/decode independent scaling — per-workload-shape recipes.\n64\t- HLR-6 [P1]: operator guidance for observing scaling events with signal attribution on both paths.\n65\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-83d800cd5acabd92","agent_id":"aecb55fe7396d6010","session_id":"04280142-99a3-46b0-81af-b1932074d3a4","uuid":"e2ab2b18-4fb4-4d77-9843-be0e5d005b39","timestamp":"2026-10-22T05:16:35.121Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a789a4d74fa1815c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8008 decomposition"}
